WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.203
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.203 --> 00:00:08.567
Hi, I'm Christian Wheeler.

00:00:08.567 --> 00:00:12.582
I teach a lot of courses on the topic of
leadership agility at Stanford GSB and

00:00:12.582 --> 00:00:16.238
these are five takeaways from my course,
spontaneous management.

00:00:16.238 --> 00:00:22.373
[MUSIC]

00:00:22.373 --> 00:00:26.741
In Silicon Valley, a lot of companies
have the motto of failing fast, failing

00:00:26.741 --> 00:00:31.456
frequently, but on a personal level, it
can be difficult for us to accept failure.

00:00:31.456 --> 00:00:35.666
We often have a fear of looking stupid in
front of other people or making mistakes,

00:00:35.666 --> 00:00:39.587
but an outsize fear of failure has
negative consequences for us as leaders.

00:00:39.587 --> 00:00:43.207
A fear of failure can make
you avoid growth situations.

00:00:43.207 --> 00:00:46.338
And it's only when we are at
the outer edge of our limits,

00:00:46.338 --> 00:00:50.450
when we are encountering that failure
possibility that we are operating at

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:51.964
the peak of our abilities.

00:00:51.964 --> 00:00:54.886
Failure is a step on the path to learning.

00:00:54.886 --> 00:00:58.047
Secondly, dwelling on failure
takes you out of the situation so

00:00:58.047 --> 00:01:01.104
you can't respond adaptively
to what the context gives you.

00:01:01.104 --> 00:01:05.201
We have a motto in the class to be
comfortable being uncomfortable.

00:01:05.201 --> 00:01:07.704
You will feel anxious in
a lot of situations, but

00:01:07.704 --> 00:01:11.062
if you can learn to ease into that
feeling without dwelling on it,

00:01:11.062 --> 00:01:13.582
you can respond most
adaptively in any context.

00:01:13.582 --> 00:01:17.885
[MUSIC]

00:01:17.885 --> 00:01:21.294
Human beings communicate through
a wide array of channels, and

00:01:21.294 --> 00:01:24.842
only one of them is the verbal channel,
the words that people say.

00:01:24.842 --> 00:01:28.667
Most in-person communication
happens through non-verbal signals.

00:01:28.667 --> 00:01:32.025
It is through these signals that
we get the layers of meaning,

00:01:32.025 --> 00:01:36.935
people's unstated beliefs, their comfort
level, their emotions, their status, and

00:01:36.935 --> 00:01:41.671
it's critical for us as leaders to learn
how to interpret these non-verbal signals.

00:01:41.671 --> 00:01:45.112
But it's also important for us to
understand that we are always giving off

00:01:45.112 --> 00:01:47.737
these non-verbal signals whether or
not we intend to.

00:01:47.737 --> 00:01:50.604
In our class, we teach you how
to broaden your range much

00:01:50.604 --> 00:01:53.837
like an actor might learn to play
different roles so that you can

00:01:53.837 --> 00:01:58.309
recruit the non-verbal behaviors that are
most adaptive to you in any given context.

00:01:58.309 --> 00:02:02.367
[MUSIC]

00:02:02.367 --> 00:02:05.506
Improvisational theater is
a foundation of this class, and

00:02:05.506 --> 00:02:08.348
improvisational theater is
all about story telling.

00:02:08.348 --> 00:02:10.354
Practically since the dawn of language,

00:02:10.354 --> 00:02:12.940
human beings have been
telling one another stories.

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:16.807
And the narrative structure is a structure
through which we give context and

00:02:16.807 --> 00:02:20.020
even our own lives a sense of meaning,
purpose, and journey.

00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:21.934
Stories are emotional.

00:02:21.934 --> 00:02:26.118
They allow the listener to develop
an empathetic response to the protagonist.

00:02:26.118 --> 00:02:29.872
And research shows that stories can
be more compelling than rhetoric and

00:02:29.872 --> 00:02:31.172
more memorable as well.

00:02:31.172 --> 00:02:34.322
You may not think of yourself
as a skilled storyteller, but

00:02:34.322 --> 00:02:36.087
you can learn these techniques.

00:02:36.087 --> 00:02:38.251
Stories have a typical structure and

00:02:38.251 --> 00:02:41.888
compelling stories have common
elements that can be taught.

00:02:41.888 --> 00:02:45.373
If you can learn to turn
your facts into stories,

00:02:45.373 --> 00:02:50.221
you can develop a deep and
enduring connection with your audience.

00:02:50.221 --> 00:02:51.472
[MUSIC]

00:02:51.472 --> 00:02:56.581
If you've read anything about improv, you
know the first rule of improv is yes, and.

00:02:56.581 --> 00:03:01.069
The yes component means that we are taking
somebody's input and incorporating it, and

00:03:01.069 --> 00:03:04.234
the and means that we are building
specifically on that idea.

00:03:04.234 --> 00:03:08.381
And it is through this process that
an improv company can take a simple

00:03:08.381 --> 00:03:11.671
suggestion like a relationship
between two people and

00:03:11.671 --> 00:03:16.200
end up with a fully creative,
dramatic, full length, 90 minute play.

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:19.768
It is through incorporating others ideas
and building on them that you can end up

00:03:19.768 --> 00:03:21.961
in creative places that
you never anticipated.

00:03:21.961 --> 00:03:26.052
Many of us are resistant to yes, and type
of thinking, because we want to contain

00:03:26.052 --> 00:03:30.218
the situation, we want to have a sense of
control over the direction that goes in.

00:03:30.218 --> 00:03:35.561
So when we can learn to cede some
of that control, to incorporate and

00:03:35.561 --> 00:03:39.969
build on others ideas,
we can generate breakthrough,

00:03:39.969 --> 00:03:42.907
innovative, creative solutions.

00:03:42.907 --> 00:03:48.174
When you learn to embrace spontaneity, to
relax into the moment, to be comfortable

00:03:48.174 --> 00:03:52.643
being uncomfortable, you can be
authentic and present in the moment.

00:03:52.643 --> 00:03:57.207
When you learn to let go of preconceived
notions you have about yourself, to let go

00:03:57.207 --> 00:04:01.638
of expectations about others and the
context, then you can be fully present and

00:04:01.638 --> 00:04:04.436
adaptively respond to what
the moment gives you.

00:04:04.436 --> 00:04:07.088
This allows you to attend
to unspoken things.

00:04:07.088 --> 00:04:10.418
It allows you to be responsive
to ideas in your environment,

00:04:10.418 --> 00:04:13.568
to see connections between
ideas that others overlook.

00:04:13.568 --> 00:04:17.784
It allows you to interact deeply and
authentically to build rapport and

00:04:17.784 --> 00:04:19.057
achieve your goals.

00:04:19.057 --> 00:04:29.057
[MUSIC]

00:04:34.177 --> 00:04:38.531
Yeah, you can't be an improviser unless
you're willing to look like a complete

00:04:38.531 --> 00:04:41.873
idiot in front of people on
an unpredictable schedule [LAUGH].

00:04:41.873 --> 00:04:51.873
[MUSIC].

